<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!--
      Invariant I1 forbids INTERNET, and that is the whole of what it forbids —
      OUTLINE.md's invariant table names no other permission. Every granting
      permission below was added by the task that needed it, is justified where it
      is declared, and is exercised by something this app actually runs.

      :app:check<Variant>Permissions enforces this against the MERGED manifest on every
      debug build: INTERNET fails it, and so does every READ_MEDIA_* except
      READ_MEDIA_AUDIO, which `music-recorder-covers` declares by name for the music
      player cover.
    -->

    <!--
      WorkManager declares this so it can reschedule jobs after a reboot. This app
      cannot use it: an import needs the VMK, the VMK exists only in memory while
      the vault is unlocked, and after a reboot the vault is locked. Work revived
      at boot could do nothing but fail.

      A permission the app cannot exercise is a permission that should not be
      declared — the manifest is the one claim about this app that anyone can check
      without trusting us. (P1-7)
    -->
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED"
        tools:node="remove" />

    <!--
      WorkManager and media3 both declare this. The app cannot exercise it: I1
      forbids INTERNET, so there is no network to have a state, and nothing here
      can act on what the answer would be.

      Removing it is safe but not free of consequence, and the consequence runs
      the wrong way. WorkManager does not throw without this permission — it
      catches the SecurityException and returns NetworkState(isConnected=false)
      permanently. So a work request that ever sets a network constraint would
      never become eligible to run: no crash, no error, just an operation stuck
      in ENQUEUED forever. :app:check<Variant>WorkConstraints fails the build if any
      app class asks for one. (P1-8)
    -->
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE"
        tools:node="remove" />

    <!--
      Play Billing declares this. I1 forbids it outright, and this is the one
      permission on the list that is not a judgement call: the whole product claim
      is that the app cannot send anything anywhere, and INTERNET is what anyone
      checking that claim reads first.

      The app cannot exercise it and neither does Billing's purchase flow. A
      purchase is bindService IPC to com.android.vending — the Play Store app owns
      the network side of it, under its own permissions, in its own process. The
      declaration is a manifest-merger artifact of a library that also supports
      configurations this app does not use.

      "Should work" is not a measurement, so P1-16 task 1 measured it on this build
      with this directive in place: connection, product query and a completed
      purchase, all recorded in OUTLINE.md P1-16 against billing-ktx 9.1.0. If a
      future version of the library genuinely needs the socket, the purchase stops
      working and the model changes — the permission does not come back. (P1-16)
    -->
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.INTERNET"
        tools:node="remove" />

    <!--
      Imports run as foreground work so the process is not reclaimed mid-import.
      That is not a nicety: PickVisualMedia's read grant is process-scoped, so an
      import that outlives its process cannot reopen its own source, whatever the
      resume logic does (measured in P1-8.5, SecurityException on the picker URI).

      Without this a multi-GB import is lost to the user swiping the app away.
      The type is literal — this is data sync, into the vault. Android 15 caps
      dataSync foreground services at six hours a day; imports are minutes.
      (P1-8.6)
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_DATA_SYNC" />

    <!--
      Intruder capture (P2-4). The two permissions the OUTLINE.md line names, and
      the first this app declares that are neither stripped nor infrastructure: they
      are requested at runtime only when a capture mode is turned on, one at a time,
      never bundled and never at onboarding. Both are exercised — a still from the
      front camera, a clip from the microphone, sealed into a vault on a failed
      unlock — so unlike the four above they carry no tools:node="remove".

      Neither is forbidden: I1 bars INTERNET, and :app:check<Variant>Permissions passes
      with these present. The core product runs
      unchanged with all capture modes off and both permissions denied, which is the
      Verify. The store listing must say what they are for, the same obligation
      FOREGROUND_SERVICE_DATA_SYNC carries.
    -->
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.CAMERA"
        tools:ignore="PermissionImpliesUnsupportedChromeOsHardware" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECORD_AUDIO" />

    <!--
      A memo keeps recording when the screen turns off (destination-settings-and-slideshow D6).
      Android lets an app keep the microphone with nothing of it on screen only inside a
      foreground service of type microphone, started while the app is visible, and that
      service must post a notification: FOREGROUND_SERVICE and its microphone type for the
      first, POST_NOTIFICATIONS — asked the first time a memo is recorded, never before — for
      the second. Refusing it does not stop the recording; the shade just has no Stop.

      MODIFY_AUDIO_SETTINGS is what "Use Bluetooth microphone" needs to route a headset's
      microphone: setCommunicationDevice from API 31, startBluetoothSco on 30. A normal
      permission, granted at install, with no dialog; used only while that setting is on and a
      memo is recording, and released when it stops.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_MICROPHONE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.MODIFY_AUDIO_SETTINGS" />

    <!--
      The music player cover (`music-recorder-covers`). It reads the device's own
      audio library through MediaStore, which is what that cover is: a player that
      opens onto a folder picker is not what any shipped player does, and the first
      screen is the one an observer is most likely to see.

      READ_MEDIA_AUDIO exists from API 33. minSdk is 30, so API 30-32 reach
      MediaStore through READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE, capped so it is never requested on a
      platform that has the narrow permission.

      Requested at runtime only from identity setup, inside the vault, never from a
      live cover: a system permission dialog reads the APPLICATION label, which is
      the vault's real name and which no alias label reaches (FORMAT.md §10).

      Neither is forbidden: I1 bars INTERNET, and :app:check<Variant>Permissions permits
      READ_MEDIA_AUDIO by exact name while still failing on every other
      READ_MEDIA_*. The core product runs unchanged with the music identity never
      chosen and both denied. The store listing must say what they are for, the same
      obligation CAMERA and RECORD_AUDIO already carry.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_MEDIA_AUDIO" />
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE"
        android:maxSdkVersion="32" />

    <!--
      androidx.biometric declares this alongside USE_BIOMETRIC. It governs the
      pre-API-28 FingerprintManager path only, and minSdk is 30 — this app cannot
      reach the code that would exercise it, on any device it runs on.

      Same rule as the two above: a permission the app cannot exercise is a
      permission it does not declare. The manifest is the one claim about this app
      that anyone can check without trusting us, and a legacy fingerprint
      permission on a biometric vault is exactly the kind of entry that makes the
      rest of the list less believable. USE_BIOMETRIC stays — it is the one the
      app actually uses. (P1-9)
    -->
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.USE_FINGERPRINT"
        tools:node="remove" />

    <!--
      `backup-to-cloud-apps`. The "Back up to" list names the cloud apps on this
      device — every app publishing a DocumentsProvider — and on API 30+ package
      visibility hides them unless the app says which intent it looks for. This is
      a query, not a permission: it grants nothing, and INTERNET stays removed
      above. Every byte a backup sends still goes through that app, under its own
      permissions, in its own process.
    -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.content.action.DOCUMENTS_PROVIDER" />
        </intent>
    </queries>

    <application
        android:name=".VaultApplication"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_toolbox"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.Vault">

        <!--
          Not exported and carrying no intent filter. The launcher entries are the
          three aliases below, which is what makes "exactly one enabled" a rule that
          can be asserted: leaving the honest identity as a filter on this activity
          would make the one component that cannot be disabled a permanent second
          entry the moment any alias was on. (P2-22)

          `landscape-rotation`. A turn is not a launch and not a departure, so the
          activity handles it in place and Compose re-lays itself out. Recreating on a
          turn stopped the old instance (auto-lock counted that as leaving), re-raised
          the cover in `onCreate`, and dropped the open item with its player.
          `uiMode` is deliberately absent: night mode still recreates. Nothing may be
          resolved once in `onCreate` and assumed to hold for one orientation.

          `app-languages` D3. A language change is handled in place for the same reason:
          a recreate would raise the cover in `onCreate` and drop every screen's state, so
          choosing a language on the Language sheet would land a cover-identity user on the
          calculator. `layoutDirection` rides with `locale` because the platform changes the
          two together.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:configChanges="orientation|screenSize|screenLayout|smallestScreenSize|locale|layoutDirection"
            android:exported="false" />

        <!--
          Launcher identity — P2-22. Three aliases on MainActivity, exactly one
          enabled at any instant, switched at runtime by LauncherIdentity through
          PackageManager.setComponentEnabledSetting and reconciled from the stored
          preference at every start.

          What these are: a choice of the name and icon on a launcher entry that
          opens the vault's ordinary unlock screen. What these are not: a disguise
          that the app then acts out. No alias declares any filter but MAIN/LAUNCHER,
          none accepts an intent from another app, and no gesture, tap count or
          arithmetic sequence authenticates anywhere behind them — the passphrase
          against the slot array remains the only thing that opens anything. A
          functional cover app behind one of these is P2-24's to add, under P2-24's
          terms; adding one here would be the mechanism `Do not build` rejects.

          android:enabled below is the shipped starting point and not a value any
          code reads: LauncherIdentity.reconcile compares against it so that a fresh
          install converges with no component write at all.
        -->
        <activity-alias
            android:name=".alias.Vault"
            android:targetActivity=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:enabled="true"
            android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_vault"
            android:label="@string/identity_vault">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity-alias>

        <!--
          The three covers. Same shape as the alias above and the same rules:
          exported with the LAUNCHER filter and no other, enabled="false" as a
          starting point that only LauncherIdentity.reconcile reads, no uses-feature.

          What is different is what they open. These three route to a cover front end
          — a working scientific calculator, a music player over the device's own
          audio, a voice recorder — whose user-chosen trigger leaves it for the
          vault's ordinary unlock screen. **No gesture behind
          them authenticates.** The passphrase against the slot array is still the
          only thing a gesture leads to, and a gesture that became the credential
          would be the ~15-bit secret `Do not build` refuses. There is no way past
          the unlock screen from a cover: the hold that brings up the music player's
          and voice recorder's dots only shows them (`cover-hold-dots`).

          A cover identity cannot be enabled until its trigger is chosen, and an
          identity whose trigger is missing reconciles back to the vault's own — see
          LauncherIdentity.resolved.
        -->
        <activity-alias
            android:name=".alias.Calculator"
            android:targetActivity=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:enabled="false"
            android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_calculator"
            android:label="@string/identity_calculator">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity-alias>

        <activity-alias
            android:name=".alias.Music"
            android:targetActivity=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:enabled="false"
            android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_music"
            android:label="@string/identity_musicplayer">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity-alias>

        <activity-alias
            android:name=".alias.Recorder"
            android:targetActivity=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:enabled="false"
            android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_recorder"
            android:label="@string/identity_voicerecorder">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity-alias>

        <!-- WorkManager declares this service; the type is ours to supply. -->
        <!--
            The recording's foreground service — destination-settings-and-slideshow D6. Not
            exported: only this app starts it, and its Stop arrives as this app's own
            PendingIntent.
        -->
        <service
            android:name=".MemoRecordingService"
            android:exported="false"
            android:foregroundServiceType="microphone" />

        <service
            android:name="androidx.work.impl.foreground.SystemForegroundService"
            android:foregroundServiceType="dataSync"
            tools:node="merge" />

        <!--
            P4-2. Serves one directory — `filesDir/shares/` — so a share container can
            be handed to a messenger by content URI.

            `exported="false"` with `grantUriPermissions="true"` is the whole security
            posture: no other app can reach this provider on its own, and one reaches a
            single container only because `ACTION_SEND` handed it a read grant for that
            URI. `manifest-hardening`'s "no exported service, receiver, or provider" is
            satisfied as written, and this registers no intent filter — receiving is
            P4-3's, not this change's.
        -->
        <provider
            android:name="androidx.core.content.FileProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.shares"
            android:exported="false"
            android:grantUriPermissions="true">
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS"
                android:resource="@xml/share_paths" />
        </provider>

    </application>

</manifest>
